*  CN / the $() INDIRECT LAW, measured on live x64 sbl (Lon s168: "use SPITBOL as an Oracle").
*  ⛔ THIS WITNESS FALSIFIES THE PREMISE THE s153 RULING REQUEST WAS PHRASED UNDER.  That truth
*  table's first row read `$("&N") -> 42 (same cell as &N: correct)` and called the third row an
*  "asymmetry" needing a seal ruling.  MEASURED, THERE IS NO SHARED CELL AND SO NOTHING TO SEAL:
*  in SPITBOL `$('&X')` names an ORDINARY VARIABLE literally spelled "&X", a namespace WHOLLY
*  DISJOINT from the keyword &X, and it never reaches keyword space in EITHER direction.
*  Receipts (x64/bin/sbl -b, this file's .ref is its verbatim output):
*    r2  $('&ANCHOR') is NULL even though the keyword reads 1 -- two different cells
*    r3  an indirect WRITE leaves the keyword UNTOUCHED (still 1) -- not a bypass, a different var
*    r5  $('&NEVERSET') = 99 is accepted SILENTLY where a direct &NEVERSET raises an error
*  ARCH-SN4-CONSTANTS's "No bypass via OPSYN/$('&X')/aliasing" is therefore VACUOUS for the $()
*  clause, not merely "narrow to writes": the seal lives on the CELL and $() addresses another one.
	&ANCHOR = 1
	OUTPUT = 'r1-direct-anchor=' &ANCHOR
	OUTPUT = 'r2-indirect-anchor=[' $('&ANCHOR') ']'
	$('&ANCHOR') = 'HELLO'
	OUTPUT = 'r3-direct-after-indirect-write=' &ANCHOR
	OUTPUT = 'r4-indirect-after-indirect-write=[' $('&ANCHOR') ']'
	$('&NEVERSET') = 99
	OUTPUT = 'r5-indirect-neverset=[' $('&NEVERSET') ']'
	OUTPUT = 'reached-end'
END
